=> Discover Log-Based Metrics in Application Insights

    -> What are Log-Based Metrics?
        - Metrics used to analyze application health and performance
        - Based on telemetry logs stored in Application Insights
        - Internally translated into Kusto Query Language (KQL)

    -> Types of Metrics in Application Insights

        -> Log-Based Metrics
            - Derived from stored telemetry events (logs)
            - Uses KQL queries behind the scenes
            - Highly detailed and flexible

        -> Standard Metrics (Preaggregated Metrics)
            - Stored as precomputed time series data
            - Aggregated during data collection
            - Optimized for performance

====================================================================================================
=> Key Difference Between Log-Based and Standard Metrics

    -> Log-Based Metrics
        - Query-time aggregation from raw events
        - High dimensionality (more filtering options)
        - Best for:
            - Deep analysis
            - Root cause investigation
            - Ad-hoc diagnostics
        - Slower compared to standard metrics

    -> Standard Metrics (Preaggregated)
        - Precomputed during data collection
        - Stored as time-series data
        - Best for:
            - Dashboards
            - Real-time monitoring
            - Alerts
        - Faster and more efficient

====================================================================================================
=> When to Use Which Metric?

    -> Use Standard Metrics When:
        - Building dashboards
        - Configuring real-time alerts
        - Need high performance queries
        - Monitoring live system health

    -> Use Log-Based Metrics When:
        - Doing deep analysis
        - Investigating failures
        - Performing ad-hoc queries
        - Need detailed dimensions

====================================================================================================
=> How Log-Based Metrics Work

    -> Data Collection
        - Application sends telemetry via:
            - SDK (manual instrumentation)
            - Auto-instrumentation

    -> Storage Model
        - All telemetry is stored as logs
        - Metrics are derived from these logs using queries

    -> Query Engine
        - Uses Kusto Query Language (KQL)
        - Enables advanced filtering and grouping

    Example:
        - Count requests to a specific URL
        - Find number of unique users per endpoint
        - Analyze exceptions per session

====================================================================================================
=> Benefits of Log-Based Metrics

    -> High Analytical Power
        - Deep visibility into application behavior
        - Supports complex queries

    -> Session-Level Insights
        - Track user journey
        - Correlate requests, exceptions, and dependencies

    -> Diagnostic Strength
        - Helps root cause analysis
        - Shows full trace of failures

====================================================================================================
=> Challenge with Log-Based Metrics

    -> High Data Volume
        - Large applications generate massive telemetry

    -> Cost & Performance Impact
        - Storing all events increases cost
        - Querying raw logs can be slower

    -> Solution Techniques
        - Sampling
        - Filtering
        - Data reduction

    -> Trade-off
        - Less data = less accuracy in log-based metrics

====================================================================================================
=> Preaggregated (Standard) Metrics

    -> What They Are
        - Metrics computed during data collection
        - Stored as summarized time series

    -> Key Advantage
        - Faster query performance
        - Lower resource usage

    -> Best For
        - Real-time dashboards
        - Alerting systems
        - High-frequency monitoring

====================================================================================================
=> SDK Behavior for Metrics

    -> Modern SDKs (.NET 2.7+)
        - Preaggregate metrics before sending
        - Reduces data ingestion cost
        - Improves efficiency

    -> Custom Metrics
        - Can be sent using GetMetric API
        - Supports preaggregation

    -> Older SDKs
        - Send raw events
        - Backend performs aggregation

====================================================================================================
=> Sampling Impact

    -> Important Insight
        - Ingestion sampling does NOT affect preaggregated metrics

    -> Reason
        - Aggregation happens before ingestion sampling

    -> Benefit
        - Ensures accuracy of standard metrics

====================================================================================================
=> Namespace Switching

    -> Purpose
        - Switch between metric types in Azure Portal

    -> Options
        - Log-based metrics namespace
        - Standard metrics namespace

    -> Tool Used
        - Metrics Explorer

====================================================================================================
=> Real-World Example

    -> E-Commerce Application

        Log-Based Metrics Used For:
            - Finding slow API calls
            - Debugging payment failures
            - Analyzing user sessions

        Standard Metrics Used For:
            - Dashboard showing request rate
            - Real-time failure alerts
            - CPU and memory monitoring

====================================================================================================
=> Key Interview Concepts

    -> Log-Based Metrics
        - Derived from logs using KQL

    -> Standard Metrics
        - Preaggregated time-series metrics

    -> KQL
        - Query language used for logs

    -> Sampling
        - Reduces telemetry volume

    -> Preaggregation
        - Improves performance and reduces cost

====================================================================================================
=> Final Summary

    - Application Insights provides two metric types:
        -> Log-Based (detailed, analytical)
        -> Standard (fast, real-time)

    - Best practice:
        - Use Standard metrics for monitoring & alerts
        - Use Log-based metrics for deep troubleshooting

=> Instrument an App for Monitoring (Application Insights)

    -> What is Instrumentation?
        - Instrumentation means enabling an application to capture telemetry data
        - Helps monitor:
            - Performance
            - Failures
            - Usage patterns
            - Dependencies

====================================================================================================
=> Two Ways to Instrument an Application

    -> 1. Automatic Instrumentation (Autoinstrumentation)
    -> 2. Manual Instrumentation

====================================================================================================
=> 1. Automatic Instrumentation (Autoinstrumentation)

    -> Definition
        - Enables telemetry collection without changing application code
        - Configured externally (agent-based approach)

    -> How it Works
        - Monitoring agent runs alongside the application
        - Automatically collects telemetry data

    -> Advantages
        - No code changes required
        - Easy to enable
        - Quick setup
        - Ideal for existing applications

    -> Limitations
        - Less configurable
        - Not supported in all languages/platforms
        - Limited customization of telemetry

    -> Best Use Cases
        - Production applications
        - Quick monitoring setup
        - Legacy applications

====================================================================================================
=> 2. Manual Instrumentation

    -> Definition
        - Instrumenting application by writing code
        - Uses SDK or OpenTelemetry APIs

    -> How it Works
        - Developer installs Application Insights SDK
        - Adds telemetry code inside application

    -> Advantages
        - Full control over telemetry
        - Highly customizable
        - Supports custom events and metrics

    -> Disadvantages
        - Requires code changes
        - Requires maintenance and SDK updates

    -> When to Use Manual Instrumentation

        -> Required Scenarios
            - Need custom events and metrics
            - Need fine control over telemetry flow
            - Autoinstrumentation not supported for language/platform

====================================================================================================
=> Application Insights SDK Approach

    -> What it Does
        - Provides library to send telemetry to Application Insights

    -> What You Need to Do
        - Install SDK package
        - Instrument:
            - Web application
            - Background services
            - JavaScript frontend

    -> Important Point
        - Application does NOT need to be hosted in Azure
        - Telemetry is sent using instrumentation key / connection string

    -> Use Cases
        - Custom business tracking
        - Advanced telemetry control
        - Fine-grained monitoring

====================================================================================================
=> OpenTelemetry Approach

    -> What is OpenTelemetry?
        - Open-source observability framework
        - Created by combining OpenCensus + OpenTracing
        - Standardized across cloud providers and APM tools

    -> Microsoft Role
        - Contributor and CNCF Platinum member
        - Supports OpenTelemetry integration in Azure Monitor

====================================================================================================
=> Mapping: Application Insights vs OpenTelemetry

    Application Insights            -> OpenTelemetry
    ----------------------------------------------------------
    Autocollectors                 -> Instrumentation libraries
    Channel                        -> Exporter
    Codeless / Agent-based         -> Autoinstrumentation
    Traces                         -> Logs
    Requests                       -> Server Spans
    Dependencies                   -> Other Span Types
    Operation ID                   -> Trace ID
    Operation Parent ID            -> Span ID

====================================================================================================
=> Key Differences Between Approaches

    -> Autoinstrumentation
        - No code changes
        - Fast setup
        - Limited customization

    -> Manual Instrumentation
        - Requires code changes
        - Highly flexible
        - Supports custom telemetry

    -> OpenTelemetry
        - Standardized observability model
        - Portable across platforms
        - Future-oriented approach

====================================================================================================
=> Real-World Example

    -> E-Commerce Application Monitoring

        Autoinstrumentation captures:
            - HTTP requests
            - Response time
            - Dependency calls

        Manual instrumentation captures:
            - "OrderPlaced" event
            - "PaymentSuccess" custom metric
            - Business-specific logs

====================================================================================================
=> When to Use What?

    -> Use Autoinstrumentation When:
        - You want quick setup
        - You don’t want to modify code
        - Standard monitoring is enough

    -> Use Manual Instrumentation When:
        - You need custom business telemetry
        - You want full control
        - You need advanced tracing or metrics

    -> Use OpenTelemetry When:
        - You want vendor-neutral observability
        - You need cloud portability
        - You are building modern distributed systems

====================================================================================================
=> Key Interview Concepts

    -> Instrumentation
        - Process of enabling telemetry collection

    -> Autoinstrumentation
        - Code-free monitoring setup

    -> Manual Instrumentation
        - SDK-based telemetry customization

    -> OpenTelemetry
        - Industry standard observability framework

    -> Telemetry Types
        - Logs
        - Metrics
        - Traces

====================================================================================================
=> Final Summary

    - Instrumentation = enabling app monitoring
    - Two approaches:
        -> Automatic (easy, no code changes)
        -> Manual (flexible, code-based)
    - Modern trend:
        -> OpenTelemetry integration for unified observability



=> Select an Availability Test (Application Insights)

    -> What are Availability Tests?
        - Used to monitor web app or website uptime and responsiveness
        - Application Insights sends HTTP/HTTPS requests at regular intervals
        - Tests are executed from multiple locations worldwide
        - No code changes required in the application

====================================================================================================
=> Purpose of Availability Tests

    -> Monitor Application Health
        - Check if application is reachable
        - Measure response time
        - Detect downtime or slow response

    -> Test External Accessibility
        - Works for:
            - Websites
            - Web apps
            - REST APIs

====================================================================================================
=> Key Limit

    -> Maximum Tests Allowed
        - Up to 100 availability tests per Application Insights resource

====================================================================================================
=> Types of Availability Tests

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
=> 1. Standard Test

    -> Definition
        - Sends a single HTTP request to the endpoint
        - Similar to legacy URL ping test but more advanced

    -> What it Validates
        - Endpoint availability
        - Response time
        - TLS/SSL certificate validity
        - HTTP request types (GET, POST, HEAD, etc.)
        - Custom headers
        - Custom request data

    -> Advantages
        - More powerful than classic ping test
        - Supports advanced validation options
        - Suitable for production monitoring

    -> Example Use Case
        - Check if login API is reachable and secure

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
=> 2. Custom TrackAvailability Test

    -> Definition
        - Developer-created availability monitoring
        - Uses TrackAvailability() method in code

    -> How it Works
        - Application runs custom test logic
        - Sends results to Application Insights

    -> Advantages
        - Fully customizable
        - Suitable for complex workflows
        - Can test multi-step transactions

    -> Example Use Case
        - End-to-end checkout flow validation
        - Multi-service dependency checks

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
=> 3. URL Ping Test (Classic)

    -> Definition
        - Legacy availability test type
        - Sends simple HTTP request to endpoint

    -> What it Checks
        - Endpoint response
        - Basic performance metrics

    -> Limitations
        - Less advanced than Standard test
        - Being retired

    -> Important Warning
        - Retirement Date: September 30, 2026
        - Existing URL ping tests will be removed
        - Migration to Standard tests is required

====================================================================================================
=> Comparison of Availability Tests

    Standard Test
        - Modern replacement for ping test
        - Supports SSL, headers, HTTP methods
        - Recommended for most scenarios

    Custom TrackAvailability
        - Fully programmable
        - Best for complex business workflows

    URL Ping Test (Classic)
        - Simple monitoring
        - Deprecated and will be retired

====================================================================================================
=> Real-World Example

    -> E-Commerce Application Monitoring

        Standard Test:
            - Check homepage availability
            - Monitor API response time

        Custom Test:
            - Validate full checkout process
            - Verify payment + order creation flow

        URL Ping (Legacy):
            - Simple homepage check (deprecated)

====================================================================================================
=> Key Benefits of Availability Tests

    -> Global Monitoring
        - Tests run from multiple geographic locations

    -> No Code Changes Required
        - Works with any public HTTP/HTTPS endpoint

    -> Early Issue Detection
        - Detect downtime or slow performance quickly

    -> Alerting Support
        - Can trigger alerts when failures occur

====================================================================================================
=> Important Interview Points

    -> Availability Test
        - Ensures application uptime and responsiveness

    -> Standard Test
        - Recommended modern availability test

    -> TrackAvailability()
        - Custom availability monitoring API

    -> URL Ping Test
        - Legacy feature, being retired in 2026

====================================================================================================
=> Final Summary

    - Availability tests monitor uptime and performance
    - Three types:
        -> Standard Test (recommended)
        -> Custom TrackAvailability Test (advanced use cases)
        -> URL Ping Test (legacy, retiring)
    - Best practice: use Standard or Custom tests for modern applications


=> Troubleshoot App Performance using Application Map (Application Insights)

    -> What is Application Map?
        - A visual topology view of distributed applications
        - Helps identify:
            - Performance bottlenecks
            - Failure hotspots
            - Dependency issues across services

====================================================================================================
=> Purpose of Application Map

    -> System-Wide Visibility
        - Shows complete application architecture
        - Displays interactions between components

    -> Performance Troubleshooting
        - Identifies slow services
        - Detects failing dependencies
        - Helps isolate root cause quickly

====================================================================================================
=> What Each Node Represents

    -> Application Component
        - Each node = one service or component
        - Examples:
            - Web API
            - Microservice
            - Background worker

    -> Dependencies
        - External services used by components
        - Examples:
            - SQL Database
            - Event Hubs
            - External APIs

    -> Node Information Includes
        - Health status (success/failure rate)
        - Performance KPIs (response time, latency)
        - Alert status

====================================================================================================
=> Key Concept: Components vs Dependencies

    -> Components
        - Independently deployable parts of application
        - Have their own telemetry and code visibility
        - Can be separate Application Insights resources

        Example:
            - Order Service
            - Payment Service
            - Inventory Service

    -> Dependencies (Observed Services)
        - External systems
        - May not be owned by your team
        - Limited or no internal telemetry access

        Example:
            - Azure SQL Database
            - Event Hubs
            - Third-party APIs

====================================================================================================
=> How Application Map Works

    -> Step 1: Telemetry Collection
        - Application Insights SDK collects dependency calls
        - HTTP calls between services are tracked

    -> Step 2: Component Discovery
        - System detects connected services automatically
        - Uses dependency relationships

    -> Step 3: Map Generation
        - Builds topology view of entire system
        - Shows multi-level architecture

====================================================================================================
=> Progressive Discovery Process

    -> Initial Load
        - Map loads basic known components

    -> Background Discovery
        - System queries for additional services
        - Expands map gradually

    -> Update Map Components Button
        - Refreshes map with newly discovered nodes
        - Useful for complex systems

====================================================================================================
=> Cloud Role Name

    -> What it is
        - Identifier used to represent components in Application Map

    -> Why it matters
        - Determines how services are grouped in the map

    -> Customization
        - Can be manually set or overridden
        - Helps improve clarity in complex architectures

    Example:
        cloud_role_name = "PaymentService"
        cloud_role_name = "OrderAPI"

====================================================================================================
=> Multi-Level Topology View

    -> Capabilities
        - Shows end-to-end distributed architecture
        - Supports large-scale systems with hundreds of components

    -> Example Flow

        User Request
            -> API Gateway
                -> Order Service
                    -> Payment Service
                        -> SQL Database

====================================================================================================
=> Navigation & Drill-Down

    -> Click on Any Component
        - View performance metrics
        - See failure rates
        - Analyze dependencies

    -> Deep Diagnostics
        - Redirects to:
            - Application Insights logs
            - Performance blade
            - Failure analysis tools
            - Azure diagnostics (SQL, etc.)

====================================================================================================
=> Integration with Azure Services

    -> Supported Services
        - Azure SQL Database
        - App Services
        - Event Hubs
        - Other Azure resources

    -> Benefits
        - Direct navigation to service diagnostics
        - Access to advisor recommendations
        - Unified troubleshooting experience

====================================================================================================
=> Real-World Example

    -> E-Commerce System

        Application Map Shows:
            - Web App (Frontend)
